==Results==
{{Crisis in Bolivia sidebox}}
Two independent vote count processes were used for the elections. The first one, ''Transmisión de Resultados Electorales Preliminares'' (TREP), is a quick count process based on photographs that is meant to provide a preliminary result on election day. The second process is the traditional physical count that takes more time to complete.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://eju.tv/2019/10/por-que-hay-dos-conteos-de-votos-en-el-tse/|title=¿Por qué hay dos conteos de votos en el TSE?|work=Eju!|language=es|date=21 October 2019|access-date=22 October 2019}}</ref><ref name="CEPR">[http://cepr.net/images/stories/reports/bolivia-elections-2019-11.pdf?v=2 What Happened in Bolivia's 2019 Vote Count? The Role of the OAS Electoral Observation Mission], the [[Center for Economic and Policy Research]], November 2019</ref>

With a preliminary vote count of 45% for incumbent president [[Evo Morales]] and 38% for his leading challenger, former president [[Carlos Mesa]], after 83% of votes were counted, neither of the conditions for a first-round win appeared likely to be met. A second-round runoff vote between those two candidates would therefore be held on 15 December.<ref name="BBC50119655">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-50119655|title=Bolivia's Evo Morales set to face first run-off|date=21 October 2019|work=BBC|access-date=21 October 2019|language=en-GB}}</ref> However, no further updates to the preliminary results were made after 19:40 hours local time on election day, which caused consternation among opposition politicians and election monitors deployed by the [[Organization of American States]] (OAS); Mesa described the suspension as "extremely serious" and spoke of manipulation; the OAS requested an explanation for the pause in the publication of the vote tally.<ref name="BBG1">{{cite news |last1=Roeder |first1=Jonathan |title=Bolivia's Morales Headed for Run-Off Vote as Rival Outperforms |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-10-21/bolivia-early-results-indicate-morales-mesa-headed-to-run-off |access-date=16 November 2019 |work=Bloomberg |date=20 October 2019}}</ref><ref name="WSJ2">{{cite news |last1=Otis |first1=John |title=Bolivian Authorities Withhold Vote Count as Chance of Evo Morales Win Fades |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/bolivian-authorities-withhold-vote-count-as-chance-of-evo-morales-win-fades-11571685487?mod=searchresults&page=2&pos=6 |access-date=16 November 2019 |work=WSJ |agency=Wall Street Journal |date=21 October 2019}}</ref> But while the vote tally was not being publicized, election staff were still observed counting votes overnight.<ref name="WSJ3">{{cite news |last1=Forero |first1=Juan |title=Count Turns Controversial in Bolivian Presidential Race |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/runoff-looks-likely-in-bolivian-presidential-race-11571632607?mod=searchresults&page=2&pos=7 |access-date=16 November 2019 |work=WSJ |publisher=Wall Street Journal |date=21 October 2019}}</ref>

After the publication of the count resumed, the OAS said it observed a "drastic and hard-to-explain change in the trend",<ref>OAS (2019b).</ref> and recommended a runoff election due to what the OAS viewed as manipulation.<ref name="Guardian">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/oct/25/bolivia-evo-morales-narrowly-wins-fourth-presidential-term|title=Bolivia: narrow win for Evo Morales announced in presidential election|last1=Collyns|first1=Dan|date=25 October 2019|work=The Guardian|access-date=16 November 2019}}</ref><ref name="WSJ1">{{cite news |last1=Otis |first1=John |title=Former President Tries to Turn the Tables on Powerful Bolivian Leader |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/former-presidenttries-to-turn-the-tables-on-powerful-bolivian-leader-11572269939?mod=searchresults&page=1&pos=19 |access-date=16 November 2019 |work=WSJ |publisher=Wall Street Journal |date=28 October 2019}}</ref>

Bolivia's [[Plurinational Electoral Organ|Supreme Electoral Tribunal]] (TSE), stated that updates to the preliminary count had been halted because the official results were starting to be released.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-50119655|title=Concern as Bolivia poll results transmission stops|work=BBC News|date=21 October 2019}}</ref> The TSE also stated the vote had taken place normally and with relatively few incidents.<ref name="BBG1"/>

On 24 October 2019, Morales officially declared outright victory following a counting process which gave him 46.83% of the vote against Mesa's 36.7%, with only few votes remaining to be counted.<ref name="outrightvictory">{{Cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/latino/bolivia-s-evo-morales-poised-win-re-election-following-controversial-n1071196|title=Bolivia's Evo Morales poised to win re-election following controversial vote count|website=NBC News|date=24 October 2019 }}</ref> Though the process was deemed controversial, Morales stated that he was still open to a second round runoff if the process later determined that he did not receive the required 10 percentage point victory margin needed in order to avoid a runoff.<ref name=outrightvictory/> Cómputo Electoral concluded its counting that very same day, with final results showing Morales with 47.07% of the vote and Mesa with 36.51%. This gave Morales a victory margin of more than 10 percentage points and thus prevented a second round runoff. This was the first election since his first win in which Morales obtained less than 50% of the vote. On the morning of 25 October, the election results were made official.<ref name=BBCDeclared/>

Some ballots, accounting for 0.01% of the electorate, were voided in the department of [[Beni Department|Beni]]. A redo session was scheduled for those affected on 3 November 2019, but the electoral commission said that those votes would not change the outcome of the presidential vote.<ref name=":1">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/oct/25/bolivia-evo-morales-narrowly-wins-fourth-presidential-term|title=Bolivia: narrow win for Evo Morales announced in presidential election|last=Collyns|first=Dan|date=25 October 2019|work=The Guardian|access-date=25 October 2019|language=en-GB|issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref name="revote">{{cite news|url=https://nytimes.com/2019/10/24/world/americas/bolivian-election-revote.html|title=Bolivian Court Orders Partial Presidential Revote|last=Karita|first=Juan|date=24 October 2019|work=The New York Times|access-date=25 October 2019|language=en-GB}}</ref> On 25 October 2019 the TSE cancelled the redo session after neither MAS nor 'Bolivia Dice No' protested the inclusion of the annulled ballots.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.erbol.com.bo/el-%C3%A1nfora-1/tse-da-por-v%C3%A1lidas-cuatro-actas-que-estaban-anuladas-en-beni-y-el-c%C3%B3mputo-lleg%C3%B3-al-100|title=TSE da por válidas cuatro actas que estaban anuladas en Beni y el cómputo llegó al 100%|date=25 October 2019|work=Educación Radiofónica de Bolivia|access-date=25 October 2019|language=es-BO}}</ref>

